@chapter Muddle Frontend Language Reference
@cindex Muddle frontend
@cindex frontend, Muddle

This chapter provides a comprehensive reference for the Muddle frontend language as
recognized by the lexer and parser in the Eightbol toolchain. Muddle is based on MIT
MDL (the MIXED Language Dialect), adapted for 8-bit and 16-bit system development.
Each language element—keywords, operators, delimiters, and literals—is described with
its purpose, syntax variations, valid ranges, and side effects when processed by the
compiler.

@menu
* Lexical Conventions::
* Control Flow Keywords::
* Definition and Assignment Keywords::
* Input/Output Keywords::
* Exception Handling Keywords::
* Operators::
* Data Structures::
* Identifier Normalization::
* Unsupported Features::
@end menu


@node Lexical Conventions
@section Lexical Conventions

Muddle keywords are case-insensitive and are matched after converting the input to
uppercase. Keywords beginning with a dot (e.g., @code{.IF}) denote control-flow
constructs, while bare words like @code{AND} serve as logical operators. All keywords
are reserved and cannot be used as identifiers within their reserved contexts.

@subsubsection Source Code Format

Muddle source code uses S-expression syntax: keywords and operators are enclosed in
parentheses, with space-separated arguments. Comments begin with a semicolon (@code{;})
in column zero and extend to the end of the line.

@example
; This is a comment
(.SET X (.ADD Y 1))   ; Inline comment
@end example

@subsubsection Whitespace

Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines, carriage returns) is ignored except as a separator
between tokens. Multiple whitespace characters are treated as a single separator.

@subsubsection Number Literals

Muddle supports multiple number literal formats for specifying integers in different
radices. All formats are case-insensitive and produce integer values in the AST.

@menu
* Decimal Numbers::
* Hexadecimal Numbers::
* Octal Numbers::
* Binary Numbers::
* Dword Numbers::
@end menu

@node Decimal Numbers
@subsubsection Decimal Numbers

Decimal literals consist of one or more decimal digits (0–9), optionally preceded by
a sign. Examples: @code{42}, @code{-123}, @code{0}, @code{+256}.

The lexer recognizes decimal numbers by their numeric characters when no radix prefix
is present. The parser converts them via @code{parse-integer} with radix 10.

@node Hexadecimal Numbers
@subsubsection Hexadecimal Numbers

Hexadecimal literals represent base-16 integers. Muddle supports five syntaxes:

@itemize
@item @code{0x@var{hexdigits}} — C-style prefix (case-insensitive): @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}
@item @code{#X@var{hexdigits}} — Lisp-style prefix: @code{#XFF}, @code{#X0A}
@item @code{h'@var{hexdigits}'} — Quoted h prefix: @code{h'FF'}, @code{h'DEADBEEF'}
@item @code{H'@var{hexdigits}'} — Uppercase variant: @code{H'FF'}
@item @code{x'@var{hexdigits}'} — Alternate x prefix: @code{x'FF'}
@end itemize

Valid digits are 0–9 and A–F (case-insensitive). Examples:
@itemize
@item @code{0xFF} = 255
@item @code{#X10} = 16
@item @code{h'FF'} = 255
@end itemize

@node Octal Numbers
@subsubsection Octal Numbers

Octal literals represent base-8 integers. Supported syntaxes:

@itemize
@item @code{0o@var octaldigits} — C-style prefix (case-insensitive): @code{0o77}, @code{0o644}
@item @code{#O@var octaldigits} — Lisp-style prefix: @code{#O77}
@item @code{o'@var octaldigits'} — Quoted o prefix: @code{o'77'}
@item @code{q'@var octaldigits'} — Alternate q prefix: @code{q'77'}
@end itemize

Valid digits are 0–7. Examples:
@itemize
@item @code{0o77} = 63
@item @code{#O10} = 8
@item @code{o'777'} = 511
@end itemize

@node Binary Numbers
@subsubsection Binary Numbers

Binary literals represent base-2 integers. Supported syntaxes:

@itemize
@item @code{0b@var{bindigits}} — C-style prefix (case-insensitive): @code{0b1010}
@item @code{#B@var{bindigits}} — Lisp-style prefix: @code{#B1010}
@item @code{b'@var{bindigits}'} — Quoted b prefix: @code{b'1010'}
@end itemize

Valid digits are 0 and 1. Examples:
@itemize
@item @code{0b11111111} = 255
@item @code{#B1010} = 10
@item @code{b'1'} = 1
@end itemize

@node Dword Numbers
@subsubsection Dword Numbers

Dword literals represent 32-bit hexadecimal values. Syntax: @code{d'@var{hexdigits}'}.
The parser uses radix 16.

@example
d'FFFFFFFF' = 4294967295
d'12345678' = 305419896
@end example

@subsubsection String Literals

String literals are enclosed in double quotes: @code{"text"}. The lexer scans characters
until the matching unescaped quote. Escape sequences (e.g., @code{\"}) are recognized
within strings.

@example
"Hello, World!"
"Line one\nLine two"
@end example

String literals evaluate to themselves; they are immutable. No side effect.

@node Control Flow Keywords
@section Control Flow Keywords

@menu
* .IF:: Conditional execution
* .ELSE:: Alternative branch in a conditional
* .THEN:: Consequent branch marker
* .ENDIF:: End of conditional block
* .WHILE:: Pre-test loop
* .ENDWHILE:: End of while loop
* .FOR:: Count-controlled loop
* .ENDFOR:: End of for loop
* .EXIT:: Early exit from a loop
* .RETURN:: Return from a routine
* .GO:: Unconditional jump
@end menu

@node .IF
@section @code{.IF}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.IF} introduces a conditional statement. It evaluates a Boolean expression; if
true, the consequent statements execute; if false, control passes to the optional
@code{.ELSE} block or continues after @code{.ENDIF}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.IF @var{condition} @var{then-form})
(.IF @var{condition} @var{then-form} @var{else-form})
(.IF @var{condition} (.THEN @var{then-form}))
(.IF @var{condition} (.THEN @var{then-form}) (.ELSE @var{else-form}))
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.IF (> X 0) (.SET RESULT 1))
(.IF (= COUNTER 0)
    (.PRINT "Counter is zero")
    (.PRINT "Counter is non-zero"))
(.IF (AND (> X 0) (< Y 100))
    (.CALL ProcessData X Y))
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@var{condition} must be a Boolean expression using comparison operators (=, /=, <, >,
<=, >=) or logical operators (AND, OR, NOT). @var{then-form} and @var{else-form} are
single statements or nested forms.

@subheading Output

The AST produces an @code{:if} node mapping to Eightbol's conditional construct.

@node .ELSE
@section @code{.ELSE}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.ELSE} provides the alternate branch for a preceding @code{.IF}. It must appear
after the @code{.THEN} block (or directly after @code{.IF} if no @code{.THEN}) and
before the terminating @code{.ENDIF}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.IF @var{cond} @var{then-form} (.ELSE @var{else-form}))
(.IF @var{cond} (.THEN @var{then-form}) (.ELSE @var{else-form}))
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.IF (> X 0)
    (.SET SIGN 1)
    (.ELSE (.SET SIGN -1)))
@end example

@subheading Output

Part of the @code{:if} node's @code{:else} slot.

@node .THEN
@section @code{.THEN}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.THEN} optionally marks the consequent branch of an @code{.IF} statement. It
groups statements within the true branch.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.IF @var{cond} (.THEN @var{stmt1} @var{stmt2} ...))
@end example

@subheading Output

Delimits the @code{:then} slot of the @code{:if} node.

@node .ENDIF
@section @code{.ENDIF}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.ENDIF} terminates a conditional block started with @code{.IF}. It matches the
nearest unmatched @code{.IF}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.IF @var{cond} @var{then-form} .ENDIF)
(.IF @var{cond} @var{then-form} (.ELSE @var{else-form}) .ENDIF)
@end example

@node .WHILE
@section @code{.WHILE}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.WHILE} creates a pre-test loop. Before each iteration, the condition is
evaluated; if true, the body executes; if false, the loop terminates.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.WHILE @var{condition} @var{body-form})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.SET I 0)
(.WHILE (< I 10)
    (.PRINT I)
    (.SET I (.ADD I 1)))
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@var{condition} is any Boolean expression. @var{body-form} is one or more statements.

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:perform :until} construct with the condition negated.

@node .ENDWHILE
@section @code{.ENDWHILE}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.ENDWHILE} terminates a loop started with @code{.WHILE}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.WHILE @var{cond} @var{body-form} .ENDWHILE)
@end example

@node .FOR
@section @code{.FOR}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.FOR} implements a count-controlled loop. The loop variable is initialized to the
start value, incremented by one each iteration, and the loop continues while the
variable is less than or equal to the end value.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.FOR @var{var} @var{start} @var{end} @var{body-form})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.FOR I 1 10
    (.PRINT I))
(.FOR J 0 (SUBTRACT N 1)
    (.CALL ProcessElement J))
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item @var{var} is a variable identifier (will be created if not exists)
@item @var{start} and @var{end} are integer expressions
@item @var{body-form} is one or more statements
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:perform :varying} construct.

@node .ENDFOR
@section @code{.ENDFOR}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.ENDFOR} terminates a loop started with @code{.FOR}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.FOR @var{var} @var{start} @var{end} @var{body-form} .ENDFOR)
@end example

@node .EXIT
@section @code{.EXIT}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.EXIT} causes immediate exit from the innermost enclosing @code{.WHILE} or
@code{.FOR} loop. Control resumes after the loop's terminating statement.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.EXIT)
(.EXIT @var{code})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.WHILE T
    (.IF (> COUNT 100) (.EXIT))
    (.SET COUNT (.ADD COUNT 1)))
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's loop exit construct.

@node .RETURN
@section @code{.RETURN}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.RETURN} ends execution of the current routine and returns control to its caller.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.RETURN)
(.RETURN @var{value})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.DEFINE ABS (X)
    (.IF (< X 0) (.RETURN (.MINUS 0 X)))
    (.RETURN X))
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:go-back} node.

@node .GO
@section @code{.GO}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.GO} performs an unconditional jump to a label. This is a legacy construct;
structured programming with @code{.IF} and @code{.WHILE} is preferred.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.GO @var{label})
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:goto} node.

@node Definition and Assignment Keywords
@section Definition and Assignment Keywords

@menu
* .DEFINE:: Declare a routine or function
* .SET:: Assign to a variable or object slot
* .GET:: Retrieve the value of a variable or property
* .PUT:: Store a value (synonym for SET)
* .CALL:: Invoke a routine
@end menu

@node .DEFINE
@section @code{.DEFINE}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.DEFINE} declares a new routine (function or method). The routine becomes
available for later calls via @code{.CALL} or direct invocation.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.DEFINE @var{name} @var{params} @var{body-form})
(.DEFINE @var{name} (@var{param1} @var{param2} ...) @var{body-form})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.DEFINE HELLO ()
    (.PRINT "Hello, World!"))

(.DEFINE SQUARE (X)
    (.RETURN (* X X)))

(.DEFINE MAX (A B)
    (.IF (> A B)
        (.RETURN A)
        (.RETURN B)))
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item @var{name} is a valid identifier
@item @var{params} is an empty list () or a list of parameter names
@item @var{body-form} is one or more statements
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:method} node.

@node .SET
@section @code{.SET}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.SET} assigns a value to a variable, object slot, or table/list element.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.SET @var{var} @var{expr})
(.SET <P> @var{obj} @var{prop}> @var{expr})
(.SET <T> @var{table} @var{index}> @var{expr})
(.SET <L> @var{list} @var{index}> @var{expr})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.SET X 42)
(.SET NAME "Alice")
(.SET TOTAL (+ (* PRICE QUANTITY) TAX))
(.SET <P> PLAYER HEALTH> 100)
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item @var{var} is a variable identifier
@item @var{expr} is any expression
@item Property and index accessors require appropriate data structures
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:move} node.

@node .GET
@section @code{.GET}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.GET} retrieves the value of a variable, property, or table/list element.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.GET @var{var})
(.GET <P> @var{obj} @var{prop}>)
(.GET <T> @var{table} @var{index}>)
(.GET <L> @var{list} @var{index}>)
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.GET X)
(.GET <P> PLAYER HEALTH>)
(.SET TEMP (.GET MYARRAY 0))
@end example

@subheading Output

Returns the fetched value; used within expressions.

@node .PUT
@section @code{.PUT}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.PUT} is a synonym for @code{.SET} provided for readability. It has identical
semantics: store a value into a variable, property, or table/list element.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.PUT @var{var} @var{expr})
(.PUT <P> @var{obj} @var{prop}> @var{expr})
@end example

@node .CALL
@section @code{.CALL}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.CALL} invokes a routine by name. Upon return, execution resumes after the call.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.CALL @var{func})
(.CALL @var{func} @var{arg1} @var{arg2} ...)
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.CALL HELLO)
(.CALL CALCULATE 10 20)
(.CALL PROCESS STRING LENGTH)
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@var{func} must be a previously defined routine. Arguments are passed positionally.

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:call} node.

@node Input/Output Keywords
@section Input/Output Keywords

@menu
* .PRINT:: Output text or values
* .DISPLAY:: Output (synonym for PRINT)
* .INPUT:: Read input values
* .ACCEPT:: Read input (synonym for INPUT)
* .DIALOGUE:: Display dialogue text
@end menu

@node .PRINT
@section @code{.PRINT}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.PRINT} outputs text or values. The output may go to a console, log file, or
other destination depending on the target platform.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.PRINT)
(.PRINT @var{expr1} @var{expr2} ...)
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.PRINT "Hello, World!")
(.PRINT "Value of X is: " X)
(.PRINT "Sum: " (+ A B) " Product: " (* A B))
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:print} node.

@node .DISPLAY
@section @code{.DISPLAY}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.DISPLAY} is a synonym for @code{.PRINT}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.DISPLAY @var{expr1} @var{expr2} ...)
@end example

@node .INPUT
@section @code{.INPUT}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.INPUT} reads values from the user or input stream into variables.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.INPUT)
(.INPUT @var{var1} @var{var2} ...)
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.INPUT)
(.INPUT NAME)
(.INPUT X Y Z)
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:input} node.

@node .ACCEPT
@section @code{.ACCEPT}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.ACCEPT} is a synonym for @code{.INPUT}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.ACCEPT @var{var1} @var{var2} ...)
@end example

@node .DIALOGUE
@section @code{.DIALOGUE}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.DIALOGUE} displays dialogue text, typically for game character speech or
narrative text.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.DIALOGUE "@var{text}")
(.DIALOGUE "@var{speaker}" "@var{text}")
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.DIALOGUE "Welcome to the game!")
(.DIALOGUE "Merchant" "Have you any wool?")
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:dialogue} node.

@node Exception Handling Keywords
@section Exception Handling Keywords

@menu
* .THROW:: Raise an exception
* .CATCH:: Handle an exception
* .FINALLY:: Cleanup code after try/catch
@end menu

@node .THROW
@section @code{.THROW}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.THROW} raises an exception or fault. Control transfers to the nearest enclosing
@code{.CATCH} block.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.THROW @var{code})
(.THROW "@var{message}")
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(.THROW 1001)
(.THROW "Invalid input")
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to Eightbol's @code{:log-fault} node.

@node .CATCH
@section @code{.CATCH}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.CATCH} handles an exception thrown by @code{.THROW}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.CATCH @var{code} @var{handler-form})
@end example

@node .FINALLY
@section @code{.FINALLY}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{.FINALLY} specifies cleanup code that runs after @code{.CATCH}, regardless of
whether an exception occurred.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(.FINALLY @var{cleanup-form})
@end example

@node Operators
@section Operators

Muddle provides arithmetic, relational, and logical operators. All operators are binary
except the unary minus and logical NOT.

@menu
* Arithmetic Operators::
* Relational Operators::
* Logical Operators::
* Operator Precedence::
@end menu

@node Arithmetic Operators
@subheading Arithmetic Operators

@table @code
@item +
Addition. Syntax: @code{expr1 + expr2}. Both operands are evaluated; result is their sum.
Example: @code{(+ X 1)} increments X.

@item -
Subtraction. Syntax: @code{expr1 - expr2}. Result is the difference.
Example: @code{(- TOTAL DISCOUNT)}.

@item *
Multiplication. Syntax: @code{expr1 * expr2}. Result is the product.
Example: @code{(* WIDTH HEIGHT)}.

@item /
Division. Syntax: @code{expr1 / expr2}. Result is the quotient. Division by zero triggers
a fault. Example: @code{(/ TOTAL COUNT)}.

@item ^
Bitwise XOR. Syntax: @code{expr1 ^ expr2}. Result is the bitwise exclusive-or.
Example: @code{(^ MASK 255)}.
@end table

@node Relational Operators
@subheading Relational Operators

@table @code
@item =
Equality. Syntax: @code{expr1 = expr2}. True if operands are numerically equal.

@item /=
Inequality. Syntax: @code{expr1 /= expr2}. True if operands differ.

@item <
Less than. Syntax: @code{expr1 < expr2}.

@item >
Greater than. Syntax: @code{expr1 > expr2}.

@item <=
Less than or equal. Syntax: @code{expr1 <= expr2}.

@item >=
Greater than or equal. Syntax: @code{expr1 >= expr2}.
@end table

@node Logical Operators
@subheading Logical Operators

@table @code
@item AND
Logical conjunction. Syntax: @code{expr1 AND expr2}. True only if both operands are true.
Short-circuit evaluation may apply.

@item OR
Logical disjunction. Syntax: @code{expr1 OR expr2}. True if either operand is true.
Short-circuit evaluation may apply.

@item NOT
Logical negation. Syntax: @code{NOT expr}. True if @var{expr} is false, and vice versa.
@end table

@node Operator Precedence
@subheading Operator Precedence

Operator precedence (from lowest to highest):
@enumerate
@item Logical OR
@item Logical AND
@item Relational (=, /=, <, >, <=, >=)
@item Addition and subtraction (+, -)
@item Multiplication and division (*, /)
@item Bitwise XOR (^)
@end enumerate

Parentheses may be used to override precedence.

@node Data Structures
@section Data Structures

@menu
* LIST:: Linked list constructor
* VECTOR:: Fixed-size array
* ARRAY:: Multi-dimensional array
@end menu

@node LIST
@section LIST

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{LIST} constructs a linked list from its arguments.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(LIST @var{item1} @var{item2} ...)
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(LIST 1 2 3)
(LIST "apple" "banana" "cherry")
(LIST X (* Y 2) Z)
@end example

@node VECTOR
@section VECTOR

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{VECTOR} constructs a fixed-size vector (one-dimensional array).

@subheading Syntax

@example
(VECTOR @var{size} @var{initial-value})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(VECTOR 10 0)
(VECTOR 256 #\space)
@end example

@node ARRAY
@section ARRAY

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{ARRAY} constructs a multi-dimensional array.

@subheading Syntax

@example
(ARRAY @var{dim1} @var{dim2} ... @var{initial-value})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
(ARRAY 10 10 0)           ; 10x10 matrix initialized to 0
(ARRAY 4 4 4 1)           ; 4x4x4 cube initialized to 1
@end example

@node Identifier Normalization
@section Identifier Normalization

The Muddle frontend converts all identifiers to kebab-case form before generating AST
nodes. This normalization ensures consistent representation across the compiler
pipeline.

@subheading Normalization Rules

@itemize
@item Uppercase letters are converted to lowercase.
@item When an uppercase letter follows a lowercase letter, a hyphen is inserted.
@item Underscores and hyphens in source are normalized to single hyphens.
@item Digits are preserved and do not trigger hyphen insertion.
@end itemize

@subheading Examples

@multitable @columnfractions 0.3 0.3 0.3
@headitem Source @tab Normalized @tab Notes
@item @code{MyVariable} @tab @code{my-variable} @tab camelCase
@item @code{HTMLParser} @tab @code{h-t-m-l-parser} @tab Acronyms get hyphens
@item @code{my_variable} @tab @code{my-variable} @tab Underscore
@item @code{variable123Name} @tab @code{variable123-name} @tab Digits preserved
@end multitable

@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The Muddle frontend does not support the following features. Attempts to use them will
result in compile-time errors:

@itemize
@item @code{PROG} — Use @code{.DEFINE} with local variables instead
@item @code{COND} — Use @code{.IF} ... @code{.ELSE} ... @code{.ENDIF} instead
@item @code{LET} — Use @code{.SET} after @code{.DEFINE} instead
@item First-class continuations
@item Dynamic scope (Muddle uses lexical scope only)
@item Object-oriented features beyond property access (@code{<P>})
@item Tail-call optimization (limited stack on 8-bit targets)
@item Floating-point literals (use BCD or scaled arithmetic)
@item Complex number support
@item Property lists (association lists are supported differently)
@end itemize

For equivalent functionality, consult the Eightbol COBOL-style constructs that provide
similar capabilities with appropriate code generation for 8-bit and 16-bit systems.

@node Summary
@section Summary

This chapter has documented the complete Muddle frontend language:

@itemize
@item Control flow: @code{.IF}, @code{.ELSE}, @code{.THEN}, @code{.ENDIF}, @code{.WHILE},
@code{.ENDWHILE}, @code{.FOR}, @code{.ENDFOR}, @code{.EXIT}, @code{.RETURN}, @code{.GO}
@item Definition: @code{.DEFINE}
@item Assignment: @code{.SET}, @code{.GET}, @code{.PUT}, @code{.CALL}
@item I/O: @code{.PRINT}, @code{.DISPLAY}, @code{.INPUT}, @code{.ACCEPT}, @code{.DIALOGUE}
@item Exceptions: @code{.THROW}, @code{.CATCH}, @code{.FINALLY}
@item Arithmetic: @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{^}
@item Relational: @code{=}, @code{/=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}
@item Logical: @code{AND}, @code{OR}, @code{NOT}
@item Number formats: decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary, dword
@item Data structures: @code{LIST}, @code{VECTOR}, @code{ARRAY}
@item Identifier normalization: kebab-case
@end itemize

For implementation details, see @file{src/frontend-muddle/muddle-lexer.lisp} and
@file{src/frontend-muddle/muddle-parser.lisp}.
